# The documents an operator may edit, and the two of them that are GENERATED.
#
# All seven are conffiles, and all seven for the same reason: an upgrade must not
# replace a file a person changed without dpkg asking. The two generated documents
# are here rather than left to dpkg's defaults because an operator is EXPECTED to
# edit one of them -- a custom DNSCrypt stamp set lives in dnscrypt-proxy.toml --
# and a generated file that dpkg silently overwrote would be a configuration the
# operator had and then lost.
#
# The seventh is watchdog.yaml, and it is here for that reason twice over. It is
# the switch for the only unattended action this package takes, and a switch that
# an upgrade could silently replace is not a switch: the operator who wrote
# `automatic: false` would get `true` back on the next upgrade of a machine whose
# resolver was working.
#
# The dispatcher hook under /etc/NetworkManager is deliberately NOT a conffile.
# It is the one file under /etc this package installs that is not a document, it
# has no content an operator can edit, and the installer removes it on uninstall
# rather than leaving it behind for dpkg to prompt about a binary.
#
# The two Cloudflare range files under /usr/share are also NOT conffiles, and
# that is the same decision with the same consequence, so it is written here
# where the next reader looks rather than only in the manual:
#
#     /usr/share/mosdns-router/cloudflare-ranges.json
#     /usr/share/mosdns-router/cloudflare-ranges.lock.json
#
# They are the ranges a selector classifies every query against, and the install
# verifies the snapshot's digest against the lock before it runs any
# transaction. A conffile pair would let two files nobody edited differ from the
# package -- and an upgrade replaces the LOCK too, so a half-re-pinned pair is
# refused rather than installed, which is the correct answer and is not one an
# operator who edited the files in place would understand.
#
# THE CONSEQUENCE, so nobody has to discover it: dpkg replaces both files with
# the new package's on every upgrade WITHOUT ASKING. A hand-re-pinned snapshot
# survives exactly until the next upgrade, and the machine is then back on the
# ranges that package was built with, with nothing having said so. The way to
# re-pin is to reinstall from wherever you install it -- which is the only route
# that produces a pair this project can account for.
#
# `mosdns-cdnctl(1)` says the same thing in its FILES section, which is where an
# operator who has just edited the pair will actually look.
/etc/mosdns/mosdns.yaml
/etc/mosdns/dnscrypt-proxy.toml
/etc/mosdns/policy.yaml
/etc/mosdns/force-ech-domains.txt
/etc/mosdns/cloudflare.txt
/etc/mosdns/cloudfront-domains.yaml
/etc/mosdns/watchdog.yaml
